The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ret = kobject_init_and_add(&type->kobj, &mdev_type_ktype, NULL,
                           "%s-%s", dev_driver_string(parent->dev),
                           group->name);
if (ret) {
    kfree(type);
}
```
Reason:
When `kobject_init_and_add()` fails, it takes a reference to the kobject, but the previous implementation incorrectly uses `kfree(type)` to free the memory associated with `type`. This does not decrement the reference count of the kobject, leading to a memory leak since the kobject remains allocated but is no longer accessible.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = kobject_init_and_add(&type->kobj, &mdev_type_ktype, NULL,
                           "%s-%s", dev_driver_string(parent->dev),
                           group->name);
if (ret) {
    kobject_put(&type->kobj);
}
```
Reason:
The patch replaces `kfree(type)` with `kobject_put(&type->kobj)`, which correctly decrements the reference count of the kobject when `kobject_init_and_add()` fails. This ensures that the memory associated with the kobject is properly cleaned up, preventing the memory leak.

